WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.081
Film Courage: What’s the most important
part of the screenwriting process that if

00:00:03.081 --> 00:00:09.110
it was taken away it would just totally make
the whole thing not so fun, you know…it

00:00:09.110 --> 00:00:10.549
would totally negate the process?

00:00:10.549 --> 00:00:12.530
&nbsp;
Shawn Christensen, Co-writer/director&nbsp;THE

00:00:12.530 --> 00:00:21.040
VANISHING OF SIDNEY HALL: For me the most
important part of the process I guess is…well,

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:24.540
I mean the most important part of any process
of writing whether it’s a screenplay or

00:00:24.540 --> 00:00:29.999
not is the dreaming. You know, the dreaming
of making another world or telling a new,

00:00:29.999 --> 00:00:38.280
fresh story, I don’t know if that is particular
to screenwriting? But again I do like the

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:45.470
cinematic format of a screenplay. I’m not
going to say that I prefer it to a novel or

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:52.280
a book, but there is an immediacy to it and
I guess if screenplays didn’t have that&nbsp;immediacy,

00:00:52.280 --> 00:01:00.530
maybe I wouldn’t be as attracted to writing.
But I like that kind of you know Hunter S.

00:01:00.530 --> 00:01:06.820
Thompson sort of like really rapid-fire way
that you can write a script. Not everyone

00:01:06.820 --> 00:01:08.430
writes screenplays like that.

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:10.830
&nbsp;
Like some people,&nbsp;MANCHESTER BY THE SEA I’m

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:17.000
sure isn’t written that way, you know? It’s
a slow, slow drive and it’s just the characters

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:27.970
are subtly and beautifully and there’s different
gears you can use to write a screenplay. And

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:32.560
sometimes you want to write a slow one and
sometimes you want a fast one and that’s

00:01:32.560 --> 00:01:34.600
I think part of the fun.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:37.620
&nbsp;
Film Courage: Is there a specific book that

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:42.250
you’ve enjoyed reading that talks about
the writing process or maybe indirectly talks

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:45.270
about it that’s not really intended for
screenwriters?

00:01:45.270 --> 00:01:48.619
&nbsp;
Shawn: Well, I read a Syd Field book when

00:01:48.619 --> 00:01:57.759
I started out. I read it and I liked some
of what he had to say. And I read How to be

00:01:57.759 --> 00:02:05.940
a whatever…for dummies or something. That
didn’t do anything for me.&nbsp; Once in awhile

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:12.320
these books work when they tell you things
to avoid.

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:14.220
&nbsp;
When you go to the store you’re going to

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:19.840
see 75 books that say How To Be A Screenwriter.
I would say the most valuable aspect of those

00:02:19.840 --> 00:02:26.390
books are often the parts of the book where
they tell you to avoid things they say stay

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:32.920
away from you know because there are clichés,
there are a lot of them, and when people in

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:41.750
the film industry read those clichés over
and over again, it has an effect on them.

00:02:41.750 --> 00:02:44.870
&nbsp;
I got my experience from reading my favorite

00:02:44.870 --> 00:02:53.580
movies or my favorite screenplays. And I remember
when I opened up the&nbsp;Syd Field&nbsp;book (I think

00:02:53.580 --> 00:02:59.510
his first one) he was talking about
CHINATOWN and how he thinks it might be one

00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:05.920
of the best screenplays of the 1970’s. And
I had just at that time gotten into CHINATOWN.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:10.840
I’d watched it like eight times in a row
I was…whatever…I was 18 years old. I had

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:16.950
just really discovered it and I loved it.
That’s what made me want to be a screenwriter

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:22.200
and then to see him say that in his prologue
it’s like Oh I want to be on this guy’s

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:28.060
and I read CHINATOWN. And I remember I read
SHAWSHANK REDEMPTION and AMERICAN BEAUTY.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:34.160
I wanted to read AMERICAN BEAUTY because (I
mean people don’t know this) but it has

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:38.560
a whole different ending than the movie. You
know, I wanted to read the shooting screenplays

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:44.980
and see how different they were from the actual
movie.

00:03:44.980 --> 00:03:52.730
I got a lot of information I felt from the
screenplay for THE PEOPLE V. LARRY FLYNT,

00:03:52.730 --> 00:04:01.290
reason being that was based on a true story
obviously. Larry Flynt I think had eight wives

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:06.950
and in the movie there is just Courtney Love
[Althea Leasure] and that was just a revelation

00:04:06.950 --> 00:04:14.110
to me that in order to make a movie work in
a certain time period, here’s how you can

00:04:14.110 --> 00:04:22.100
cheat the numbers and still be mostly true.
That was obviously a big cheat but THE PEOPLE

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:29.090
V. LARRY FLYNT and SHAWNSHANK were big ones
for me (I learned a lot) about the formatting

00:04:29.090 --> 00:04:34.710
of the script and you know how much action
and how little action you need when you’re

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:38.460
at your heading and things like that, the
formatting is very important, you know the

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:42.350
aesthetic is very important and I basically
learned by reading screenplays.

